1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,343
Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,343 --> 00:00:22,865
Tonight we are travelling to Alexandria, on the coast of Egypt, about two hundred and thirty years before the common era, to visit the most famous library of the ancient world.

3
00:00:22,865 --> 00:00:25,355
We will not be hurrying through it.

4
00:00:25,355 --> 00:00:36,738
We will follow one single book, a rolled-up scroll, on its slow journey from a ship in the harbour to a quiet shelf, and then up onto a rooftop under the stars.

5
00:00:38,538 --> 00:00:41,575
So let yourself sink into your pillow.

6
00:00:41,575 --> 00:00:45,171
Let your jaw loosen and your hands rest open.

7
00:00:45,171 --> 00:00:55,879
Breathe in slowly, as if you could smell the warm sea air and the salt drying on the harbour stones, and breathe out even more slowly.

8
00:00:55,879 --> 00:00:56,358
Again.

9
00:00:56,358 --> 00:01:02,111
Your only task tonight is to listen, or not to listen, just as you like.

10
00:01:03,912 --> 00:01:08,046
If the story slips away from you for a while, let it go.

11
00:01:08,046 --> 00:01:16,167
The scroll will carry on its journey without you, and the library will still be there whenever you drift back.

12
00:01:16,167 --> 00:01:27,314
And if you fall asleep before the end, then the stars will simply keep shining over Alexandria, and that is a perfectly good way for tonight to finish.

13
00:01:37,114 --> 00:01:46,746
The sun has just gone down behind the low line of the coast, and the sky over the sea is turning from gold to a deep, clear blue.

14
00:01:46,746 --> 00:01:59,290
A merchant ship from the island of Rhodes is coming slowly in towards the harbour, her big square sail half lowered, her steersman leaning on the long oar at the stern.

15
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:04,442
Ahead of her, on its island, the great lighthouse is already burning.

16
00:02:06,242 --> 00:02:16,139
It is called the Pharos, after the little island on which it stands, and it is the tallest building most of these sailors have ever seen.

17
00:02:16,139 --> 00:02:29,648
It rises in three parts, one above the other: a broad square tower at the bottom, then an eight-sided tower, then a slim round one at the very top, where a fire is kept burning all night.

18
00:02:29,648 --> 00:02:35,789
On the summit stands a statue, often said to be Zeus the Saviour, looking out to sea.

19
00:02:37,589 --> 00:02:52,326
The lighthouse was begun under the first King Ptolemy and finished under his son, about fifty years before tonight, and it is said to have been designed by an architect called Sostratus of Cnidus.

20
00:02:52,326 --> 00:03:02,176
To a ship far out at sea, the coast of Egypt here is low and flat and hard to see, with sandbanks and rocks hidden in the shallows.

21
00:03:02,176 --> 00:03:06,687
The Pharos shows the way in, smoke by day and fire by night.

22
00:03:08,487 --> 00:03:15,683
As the ship comes nearer, you can see the long causeway that joins the island to the mainland.

23
00:03:15,683 --> 00:03:26,323
The Alexandrians call it the Heptastadion, which means seven stadia, because it is about seven stadia long, a little more than a kilometre.

24
00:03:26,323 --> 00:03:37,117
It divides the water into two harbours, one on each side, and tonight the ship is steering for the larger one, the Great Harbour on the east.

25
00:03:38,917 --> 00:03:50,499
Below decks, the cargo is packed tight in the hold: tall clay jars of Rhodian wine sealed with stoppers, baskets of dried figs, bundles of fine cloth.

26
00:03:50,499 --> 00:04:02,312
And in the captain's own cabin, in a small cedar chest, lie a few rolled books that he bought in the market at Rhodes, to read on the long nights at sea.

27
00:04:02,312 --> 00:04:06,096
One of them is the book we are following tonight.

28
00:04:07,896 --> 00:04:15,521
It is a copy of a poem called the Phaenomena, written by a poet named Aratus about forty years earlier.

29
00:04:15,521 --> 00:04:27,366
It describes the stars and constellations of the night sky, where they rise and where they set, and the signs of the weather that sailors and farmers watch for.

30
00:04:27,366 --> 00:04:36,546
It has become one of the best-loved poems in the Greek world, and the captain likes to read it on deck when the sea is calm.

31
00:04:38,346 --> 00:04:53,945
The ship slides in past the lighthouse, so close now that you can hear the soft roar of the fire high above, and see the glow of it reflected in the dark water, trembling and breaking up with every small wave.

32
00:04:53,945 --> 00:04:55,886
The sailors furl the sail.

33
00:04:55,886 --> 00:04:58,797
The oars come out for the last stretch.

34
00:04:58,797 --> 00:05:05,738
And slowly, gently, the ship comes alongside the stone quay, and the ropes are thrown ashore.

35
00:05:15,538 --> 00:05:27,401
On the quay, a few lamps are already lit outside the customs house, and men are waiting with wax tablets and tally sticks to record what each ship has brought.

36
00:05:27,401 --> 00:05:32,698
Egypt under the Ptolemies is a country that likes to write things down.

37
00:05:32,698 --> 00:05:42,845
Every jar of wine, every bale of cloth, every sack of grain is counted, weighed and taxed, and the clerks have been doing it since dawn.

38
00:05:44,645 --> 00:05:49,219
The Great Harbour is busy by day, but at dusk it grows calm.

39
00:05:49,219 --> 00:06:03,171
On the far side of the causeway lies the western harbour, which the Alexandrians call Eunostos, the harbour of the happy return, a lovely name for a place where tired ships come home.

40
00:06:03,171 --> 00:06:13,082
Between the two, small boats pass to and fro under the arches of the causeway, and the water slaps softly against the stone piers.

41
00:06:14,882 --> 00:06:20,132
But tonight, among the officials, there is someone a little different.

42
00:06:20,132 --> 00:06:36,557
He is a young assistant from the library, with ink on his fingers and a satchel over his shoulder, and his job is to ask a question that is asked of every ship that comes into this harbour: are there any books on board?

43
00:06:38,357 --> 00:06:43,183
The story goes that the kings of Alexandria gave an unusual order.

44
00:06:43,183 --> 00:06:49,179
Any book found on a ship in the harbour was to be taken to the library and copied.

45
00:06:49,179 --> 00:07:00,074
The copy was handed back to the owner, and the original was kept, and placed on the shelves with a small note saying that it had come from the ships.

46
00:07:00,074 --> 00:07:09,141
The tale was told centuries later by a famous doctor called Galen, and whether every part of it is true, nobody now can say.

47
00:07:10,941 --> 00:07:17,713
There is another story Galen told, about the third King Ptolemy, the one who is reigning tonight.

48
00:07:17,713 --> 00:07:29,373
He is said to have asked the city of Athens to lend him its official copies of the great tragedies of Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides, so that they could be copied.

49
00:07:29,373 --> 00:07:34,051
Athens agreed, but only after he promised a huge deposit of silver.

50
00:07:34,051 --> 00:07:42,429
When the copies were made, the king kept the originals, sent back the new copies, and simply let Athens keep the silver.

51
00:07:44,229 --> 00:07:52,874
So tonight, the captain from Rhodes is not surprised when the young man from the library politely asks about books.

52
00:07:52,874 --> 00:07:57,534
He goes down to his cabin and comes back with the cedar chest.

53
00:07:57,534 --> 00:08:12,794
The assistant lifts out each scroll, reads the little tag hanging from its end, and writes the titles carefully on his tablet: a book of fables, a speech by a famous orator, and the Phaenomena of Aratus.

54
00:08:14,594 --> 00:08:19,211
The captain will have his books again, he is told, in a few days.

55
00:08:19,211 --> 00:08:20,703
He shrugs and smiles.

56
00:08:20,703 --> 00:08:33,844
He has heard about the library, as everyone who sails in this sea has heard about it, and there is a kind of pride, he thinks, in a book of his resting for a while among so many others.

57
00:08:33,844 --> 00:08:40,450
The assistant thanks him, wraps the scrolls in a soft cloth, and slips them into his satchel.

58
00:08:42,250 --> 00:08:54,183
He walks away along the quay, past the moored ships knocking gently against one another, past the stacked amphorae and the coils of rope and the sleeping dogs.

59
00:08:54,183 --> 00:09:00,112
Behind him the lighthouse burns on, and its light follows him across the water.

60
00:09:00,112 --> 00:09:07,317
Ahead of him, beyond the warehouses, the city of Alexandria is lighting its lamps for the night.

61
00:09:17,117 --> 00:09:19,134
Alexandria is a young city.

62
00:09:19,134 --> 00:09:40,207
It was founded about a hundred years ago by Alexander the Great, who chose this strip of land between the sea and a great lake, and it was laid out by his architect, Dinocrates, on a neat grid of straight streets crossing one another at right angles, like the lines on a board game.

63
00:09:40,207 --> 00:09:44,914
Even in the dark, you would find it hard to lose your way here.

64
00:09:46,714 --> 00:09:54,824
The assistant turns into the greatest of those streets, the one that later writers will call the Canopic Way.

65
00:09:54,824 --> 00:10:13,722
It runs straight through the whole city from west to east, and it is astonishingly wide, wide enough for several carts to pass side by side, with long colonnades running along each edge, where people can walk in the shade by day and under lamps by night.

66
00:10:15,522 --> 00:10:19,005
At this hour, the street is calm but not empty.

67
00:10:19,005 --> 00:10:22,117
Shopkeepers are putting up their shutters.

68
00:10:22,117 --> 00:10:26,341
A man sells little cakes of sesame and honey from a tray.

69
00:10:26,341 --> 00:10:31,899
Two old men sit on a step playing a game with pebbles on a scratched board.

70
00:10:31,899 --> 00:10:44,941
You hear Greek spoken, and Egyptian, and Aramaic, and the soft voices of women calling children in from the street, in a city where many peoples have come to live side by side.

71
00:10:46,741 --> 00:11:03,528
The houses here are built of pale limestone and plastered brick, and most of them have flat roofs, where families sleep in the hottest months of summer, and dry their washing, and sit in the evening to catch the cool wind off the sea.

72
00:11:03,528 --> 00:11:13,429
On some of those roofs, tonight, you can see the small flicker of an oil lamp, and the shapes of people sitting together, talking quietly.

73
00:11:15,229 --> 00:11:28,987
Somewhere near the heart of the city, it is said, lies the tomb of Alexander himself, whose body was brought here many years ago by the first Ptolemy, and laid in a splendid tomb.

74
00:11:28,987 --> 00:11:31,139
The assistant does not stop.

75
00:11:31,139 --> 00:11:33,752
He has passed it a thousand times.

76
00:11:33,752 --> 00:11:42,898
But he lowers his voice a little as he walks by, the way people do when they pass a place that feels old and important.

77
00:11:44,698 --> 00:11:56,248
Further off, on a low hill in the south-west of the city, stands the temple of Serapis, a god who is part Greek and part Egyptian, honoured by both peoples.

78
00:11:56,248 --> 00:12:08,168
Within its walls the kings have placed a second, smaller collection of books, a daughter library, open to readers who are not among the scholars of the Mouseion.

79
00:12:08,168 --> 00:12:13,498
Its lamps, too, are lit tonight, small points of light on the dark hill.

80
00:12:15,298 --> 00:12:27,967
The air smells of the sea and of woodsmoke, of baking bread, and faintly of the lake behind the city, where boats bring grain and wine and papyrus down from the Nile.

81
00:12:27,967 --> 00:12:34,606
A breeze from the north stirs the dust along the street and makes the lamp flames lean.

82
00:12:34,606 --> 00:12:42,925
It is the wind that keeps Alexandria cooler than the rest of Egypt, and the people here are very proud of it.

83
00:12:44,725 --> 00:12:53,973
At last the assistant turns aside, towards the royal quarter near the sea, where the palaces of the kings stand among gardens.

84
00:12:53,973 --> 00:13:03,734
Here the streets are quieter, and the walls are higher, and there are guards at the gates who know him by sight and nod as he passes.

85
00:13:03,734 --> 00:13:11,221
A little further on, behind a long wall, there is a garden full of trees, and the soft sound of water.

86
00:13:21,021 --> 00:13:25,296
The library is not a single building standing on its own.

87
00:13:25,296 --> 00:13:37,297
It belongs to a larger place called the Mouseion, the shrine of the Muses, the nine goddesses of poetry, music, history, astronomy and all the arts of learning.

88
00:13:37,297 --> 00:13:39,848
Our own word museum comes from it.

89
00:13:39,848 --> 00:13:49,224
Here, under the protection of the kings, scholars from all over the Greek world come to live, and read, and think, and write.

90
00:13:51,024 --> 00:13:58,853
The geographer Strabo, who visited Alexandria about two hundred years after tonight, described what he saw.

91
00:13:58,853 --> 00:14:19,269
There was a covered walk, a peripatos, where scholars could stroll as they talked, a curved hall with stone seats, called an exedra, where they could sit together and argue, and a large house containing the common dining hall, where the men of learning took their meals together.

92
00:14:21,069 --> 00:14:30,385
The assistant walks along the covered colonnade now, between the slender columns, with the garden open to the sky on his left.

93
00:14:30,385 --> 00:14:33,490
Little lamps hang from the beams overhead.

94
00:14:33,490 --> 00:14:48,056
In the middle of the garden there is a long pool, so still that the moon lies in it like a silver coin, and around it grow date palms, their great fronds rustling softly in the breeze from the sea.

95
00:14:49,856 --> 00:14:54,194
The scholars here do not have to worry about earning a living.

96
00:14:54,194 --> 00:15:02,521
They are fed at the king's table and given a salary, and they are free to spend their days studying whatever they wish.

97
00:15:02,521 --> 00:15:05,460
Not everyone thought this was a good idea.

98
00:15:05,460 --> 00:15:17,845
A sharp-tongued poet of the time joked that in Egypt, a crowd of bookish scribblers were being fattened up in the bird-cage of the Muses, quarrelling endlessly with one another.

99
00:15:19,645 --> 00:15:22,404
Tonight, the quarrels seem to be over.

100
00:15:22,404 --> 00:15:33,729
In the exedra, two old men are sitting on the curved stone bench, wrapped in their cloaks, talking slowly about the meaning of a single word in an old poem.

101
00:15:33,729 --> 00:15:42,513
A younger man walks alone along the colonnade, reading from a scroll by the light of the lamps, his lips moving silently.

102
00:15:42,513 --> 00:15:48,829
Somewhere a door opens, and the sound of soft laughter drifts out from the dining hall.

103
00:15:50,629 --> 00:15:55,603
The scholars of the Mouseion study an astonishing range of things.

104
00:15:55,603 --> 00:16:00,352
Some are poets, and some edit the poems of Homer, line by line.

105
00:16:00,352 --> 00:16:08,341
Some are mathematicians, following in the footsteps of Euclid, who taught here a generation or two before.

106
00:16:08,341 --> 00:16:11,883
Some are doctors, who study how the body works.

107
00:16:11,883 --> 00:16:19,194
And some, like the man our scroll is going to meet tonight, try to measure the earth and the sky.

108
00:16:20,994 --> 00:16:31,902
The assistant stops at the edge of the pool for a moment, as he often does at the end of a long day, and watches the moon trembling in the water.

109
00:16:31,902 --> 00:16:35,664
A frog plops softly into the reeds at the far end.

110
00:16:35,664 --> 00:16:37,168
A palm frond creaks.

111
00:16:37,168 --> 00:16:46,722
Then he turns, climbs three shallow steps, and goes in through a tall doorway, into the lamplit rooms where the books are made.

112
00:16:56,522 --> 00:17:07,274
Every book in this library is a roll of papyrus, and before we watch our scroll being copied, it is worth knowing where papyrus comes from.

113
00:17:07,274 --> 00:17:18,102
It grows in the marshes of the Nile, a tall reed with a smooth green stem, taller than a man, crowned with a soft, feathery tuft at the top.

114
00:17:18,102 --> 00:17:24,213
Egypt has been making writing material from it for nearly three thousand years.

115
00:17:26,013 --> 00:17:32,779
To make a sheet, the workers cut the stems into lengths and peel away the green rind.

116
00:17:32,779 --> 00:17:38,112
Then they slice the pale, spongy pith inside into long thin strips.

117
00:17:38,112 --> 00:17:51,485
They lay the strips side by side on a flat board, all running the same way, and then lay a second layer across them, at right angles, like the threads of a woven cloth.

118
00:17:53,285 --> 00:18:01,253
The two layers are pressed and beaten gently with a mallet, and the plant's own sap helps hold them together.

119
00:18:01,253 --> 00:18:11,707
Then the sheet is left to dry in the sun, and afterwards polished smooth with a stone or a shell, until the surface is fine enough to write on.

120
00:18:11,707 --> 00:18:17,264
The best sheets are pale and even, the colour of honey held up to the light.

121
00:18:19,064 --> 00:18:24,603
A single sheet is not very big, perhaps the size of a page in a large book.

122
00:18:24,603 --> 00:18:33,689
So the sheets are glued edge to edge, often about twenty of them, to make one long strip, which is rolled up around itself.

123
00:18:33,689 --> 00:18:37,529
That roll is what the Greeks call a biblion, a book.

124
00:18:37,529 --> 00:18:46,541
When you read, you hold it in both hands, unrolling it with one hand and rolling it up with the other, a column at a time.

125
00:18:48,341 --> 00:18:54,690
Papyrus is one of the great exports of Egypt, shipped to every port around the sea.

126
00:18:54,690 --> 00:19:14,808
A later story, told by the Roman writer Pliny, says that when a rival library grew up at Pergamon, in Asia, a king of Egypt stopped papyrus from being sent there, and so the people of Pergamon perfected a writing material of their own, made from fine animal skin.

127
00:19:14,808 --> 00:19:18,250
We still call it parchment, after their city.

128
00:19:20,050 --> 00:19:29,906
In the room where the assistant has arrived, there are fresh rolls of blank papyrus stacked on a shelf, each one tied with a thread.

129
00:19:29,906 --> 00:19:32,296
Nearby stand little pots of ink.

130
00:19:32,296 --> 00:19:41,256
The black ink is made from soot, scraped from lamps, mixed with gum and water, and it sits in the pots like dark velvet.

131
00:19:41,256 --> 00:19:46,632
There is red ink too, for headings and corrections, made from red earth.

132
00:19:48,432 --> 00:20:00,738
The pens are made of reed, a thinner reed than the papyrus, cut to a point with a small knife and split at the tip, so that the ink flows down into the point.

133
00:20:00,738 --> 00:20:07,359
A good copyist cuts his own pens, and recuts them many times a day as they wear down.

134
00:20:07,359 --> 00:20:13,434
In a small cup on the table, a few spare reeds wait, their tips stained black.

135
00:20:15,234 --> 00:20:22,208
The room smells of lamp oil, of dust, and of the faintly sweet, grassy scent of papyrus.

136
00:20:22,208 --> 00:20:27,438
It is warm in here, because the lamps have been burning for hours.

137
00:20:27,438 --> 00:20:39,405
The assistant unwraps the cloth from his satchel, lays the three scrolls from the ship on the long table, and goes to find the copyist on duty tonight.

138
00:20:49,205 --> 00:20:56,018
The copyist is a quiet, grey-haired man who has been copying books here for most of his life.

139
00:20:56,018 --> 00:21:05,981
He looks at the three scrolls, chooses the Phaenomena first, because it is short and he knows it well, and settles himself at the table.

140
00:21:05,981 --> 00:21:15,872
A lamp stands at his elbow, its little flame leaning one way and then the other, and a fresh roll of papyrus lies open in front of him.

141
00:21:17,672 --> 00:21:23,525
He unrolls the ship's copy beside it, holding it flat with two smooth stones.

142
00:21:23,525 --> 00:21:29,987
Then he dips his reed into the ink, taps it gently on the rim of the pot, and begins.

143
00:21:29,987 --> 00:21:41,998
He writes in neat capital letters, one after another, with no spaces between the words and almost no punctuation, in a narrow column that runs down the sheet.

144
00:21:41,998 --> 00:21:46,408
When one column is finished, he starts the next beside it.

145
00:21:48,208 --> 00:22:02,515
He works slowly and steadily, in the soft scratching of the reed, saying the words very quietly to himself as he goes, because in this world most people read aloud, even when they are alone.

146
00:22:02,515 --> 00:22:10,572
Now and then he stops, and leans closer, and looks hard at a word in the ship's copy that seems odd to him.

147
00:22:10,572 --> 00:22:14,789
Then he makes a tiny mark in the margin, and carries on.

148
00:22:16,589 --> 00:22:26,599
Copying by hand is a delicate business, and every copy brings the chance of new mistakes: a line skipped, a word misread, a letter changed.

149
00:22:26,599 --> 00:22:37,472
So the library's scholars spend a great deal of their time comparing different copies of the same work, to work out which words the author truly wrote.

150
00:22:37,472 --> 00:22:47,842
A book that arrives from the ships may hold a better reading than the copies already on the shelves, or a worse one, and both are worth knowing.

151
00:22:49,642 --> 00:22:56,440
Around the room, other copyists are working too, each in their own small pool of lamplight.

152
00:22:56,440 --> 00:22:58,979
One is copying a book of medicine.

153
00:22:58,979 --> 00:23:07,047
Another is checking a finished roll against its original, running his finger down the columns, line by line.

154
00:23:07,047 --> 00:23:15,114
A boy sweeps the floor near the door with a soft broom, and moves the lamps closer to the men who need them.

155
00:23:16,914 --> 00:23:21,100
Near midnight, the copyist reaches the end of the poem.

156
00:23:21,100 --> 00:23:32,745
He writes its title at the end, as was the custom, then counts the lines and notes the number, so that anyone can check later that nothing has been lost.

157
00:23:32,745 --> 00:23:42,792
He lets the ink dry, blowing on it gently, and then rolls up the new copy, slowly and carefully, from the end back to the beginning.

158
00:23:44,591 --> 00:23:51,879
The new copy will go back to the captain from Rhodes, and the old one from the ship will stay here.

159
00:23:51,879 --> 00:23:59,977
The copyist ties a small tag to its end, with the title written on it, and the name of the place it came from.

160
00:23:59,977 --> 00:24:08,442
Then he lays it aside for the librarians, stretches his stiff fingers, and reaches for the next scroll in the pile.

161
00:24:18,242 --> 00:24:22,512
Where does a single scroll go, in a library with so many?

162
00:24:22,512 --> 00:24:26,483
Nobody knows exactly how many books the library held.

163
00:24:26,483 --> 00:24:37,495
Ancient writers gave enormous numbers, of hundreds of thousands of rolls, while many modern historians think it more likely held tens of thousands.

164
00:24:37,495 --> 00:24:47,533
But even tens of thousands of rolls, all looking much the same from the outside, need a careful system, or they would be lost forever.

165
00:24:49,333 --> 00:24:58,949
The system was built by a poet and scholar named Callimachus, who came here from the city of Cyrene, further along the African coast.

166
00:24:58,949 --> 00:25:03,866
He made a vast catalogue called the Pinakes, which means the Tables.

167
00:25:03,866 --> 00:25:07,698
It filled about a hundred and twenty rolls by itself.

168
00:25:07,698 --> 00:25:19,989
It listed authors under their subjects, such as poetry, history, law, philosophy or medicine, with a few words about each author's life, and the first line of every work.

169
00:25:21,789 --> 00:25:34,979
It was one of the very first library catalogues in history, and the idea of listing the first line, so that two books with the same title could be told apart, was a clever one.

170
00:25:34,979 --> 00:25:38,126
Many later libraries borrowed his methods.

171
00:25:38,126 --> 00:25:44,871
And yet, for all his work, Callimachus was probably never the head of the library himself.

172
00:25:44,871 --> 00:25:50,866
He was a poet first, and his own poems were famous for being short and polished.

173
00:25:52,666 --> 00:25:55,527
The heads of the library were others.

174
00:25:55,527 --> 00:26:03,644
The first was Zenodotus, who worked on the poems of Homer, comparing copies and marking lines he doubted.

175
00:26:03,644 --> 00:26:11,144
Then came the poet Apollonius of Rhodes, who wrote a long poem about Jason and the Golden Fleece.

176
00:26:11,144 --> 00:26:21,039
And now, on this evening, the head of the library is a man from Cyrene called Eratosthenes, of whom we will hear more very soon.

177
00:26:22,839 --> 00:26:29,703
The librarian on duty tonight takes the scroll of Aratus to the hall where the poetry is kept.

178
00:26:29,703 --> 00:26:39,194
It is a long, high room, lined from floor to ceiling with wooden shelves divided into square compartments, like a great honeycomb.

179
00:26:39,194 --> 00:26:52,045
In each compartment lie a few rolls, their ends facing outwards, and from many of them hangs a little tag, so that a reader can see what each roll is without having to open it.

180
00:26:53,845 --> 00:27:06,302
The librarian holds his lamp up to the shelves and walks slowly down the hall, reading the tags as he goes, until he finds the place where the other copies of Aratus lie.

181
00:27:06,302 --> 00:27:08,574
There are several here already.

182
00:27:08,574 --> 00:27:14,217
He lays the new one beside them, gently, so that its tag hangs down straight.

183
00:27:14,217 --> 00:27:21,471
Later, scholars will come and compare them all, word by word, to see what each copy can teach them.

184
00:27:23,271 --> 00:27:25,349
The hall is very quiet now.

185
00:27:25,349 --> 00:27:27,119
The lamp hisses softly.

186
00:27:27,119 --> 00:27:33,582
Somewhere far off a door closes, and the sound travels down the long room and fades.

187
00:27:33,582 --> 00:27:50,663
The tags on the shelves stir slightly in the night air from a high window, thousands of little labels, each one standing for a voice from the past, sleeping in the dark, waiting patiently for someone to come and unroll it.

188
00:28:00,463 --> 00:28:03,582
But our scroll's journey is not quite over.

189
00:28:03,582 --> 00:28:09,021
Before dawn, the head of the library himself will borrow it, for one night.

190
00:28:09,021 --> 00:28:16,491
Eratosthenes is a man of many interests: a poet, a geographer, a mathematician, a student of the stars.

191
00:28:16,491 --> 00:28:31,866
Some of the other scholars teased him with the nickname Beta, the second letter of the alphabet, because he was second best in every subject, and yet his work would be remembered for more than two thousand years.

192
00:28:33,666 --> 00:28:37,833
The story of his most famous measurement goes like this.

193
00:28:37,833 --> 00:28:46,538
He had heard that far to the south, at the town of Syene, near the first cataract of the Nile, there was a deep well.

194
00:28:46,538 --> 00:28:57,177
At noon on the longest day of the year, the sun shone straight down into it, lighting the water at the bottom, so that no shadow could be seen.

195
00:28:57,177 --> 00:29:00,674
The sun, at that moment, was directly overhead.

196
00:29:02,474 --> 00:29:09,637
But here in Alexandria, at noon on the same day, an upright stick still cast a short shadow.

197
00:29:09,637 --> 00:29:19,058
By measuring that shadow, Eratosthenes found that the sun's rays fell at an angle of about one fiftieth of a full circle.

198
00:29:19,058 --> 00:29:31,359
If the earth was round, as Greek scholars already believed, then the distance from Alexandria to Syene must be one fiftieth of the whole way around the world.

199
00:29:33,159 --> 00:29:39,241
The distance between the two places was reckoned at about five thousand stadia.

200
00:29:39,241 --> 00:29:49,019
So Eratosthenes multiplied by fifty, and found that the whole earth must be about two hundred and fifty thousand stadia around.

201
00:29:49,019 --> 00:30:03,261
We do not know exactly how long his stadion was, but by most reckonings his answer was remarkably close to the truth, made with nothing more than a stick, a shadow, and careful thought.

202
00:30:05,061 --> 00:30:07,950
Tonight, there is no shadow to measure.

203
00:30:07,950 --> 00:30:20,395
Eratosthenes has climbed up to the flat roof of the building, as he likes to do on clear evenings, with a cushion, a small lamp, and the scroll of Aratus under his arm.

204
00:30:20,395 --> 00:30:32,247
Up here, there is a bronze bowl set on a stone pillar, with a little pointer standing up inside it, a kind of sundial that measures the angle of the sun by day.

205
00:30:32,247 --> 00:30:34,543
By night it holds a little dew.

206
00:30:36,343 --> 00:30:53,481
From the roof, you can see the whole city spread out below, pale and silent under the moon: the flat roofs, the lines of the long streets, the dark shapes of the palace gardens, and the lamps along the Canopic Way, a long dotted line of gold.

207
00:30:53,481 --> 00:31:05,378
Beyond the roofs lies the harbour, and beyond that, on its island, the lighthouse fire burns steadily, small and bright as a star that has come down to rest on the sea.

208
00:31:07,178 --> 00:31:17,258
Eratosthenes sets down his lamp and his cushion, lowers himself onto the roof with a small sigh, and pulls his cloak around his knees.

209
00:31:17,258 --> 00:31:33,055
He unrolls the poem a little way, holding it up to the lamp, and then looks up at the sky, and back to the scroll, and back to the sky again, comparing the words with the stars, the way a careful scholar would.

210
00:31:42,855 --> 00:31:49,248
The poem begins with Zeus, as many Greek poems do, and then it turns to the sky.

211
00:31:49,248 --> 00:32:01,234
Aratus describes the two Bears that circle the pole of the heavens, never setting below the sea, with a long Dragon winding between them like a river.

212
00:32:01,234 --> 00:32:03,712
Sailors steer by them, he says.

213
00:32:03,712 --> 00:32:10,823
Eratosthenes finds them now, low in the north over the dark water, just as the poem says.

214
00:32:12,623 --> 00:32:27,329
Then the poem walks slowly around the sky, from one group of stars to the next: the Herdsman following the Bear, the Crown shining like a circle of jewels, the Lyre, the Swan with its wings outstretched.

215
00:32:27,329 --> 00:32:30,371
Aratus did not invent these star pictures.

216
00:32:30,371 --> 00:32:37,615
Many were already very old, coming from the Greeks before him and from the star watchers of Babylon.

217
00:32:37,615 --> 00:32:42,469
But he set them into gentle verse, so that anyone could learn them.

218
00:32:44,269 --> 00:32:57,301
Later, it is said, Eratosthenes himself would write a little book about the constellations, telling the old stories of how each figure came to be placed among the stars.

219
00:32:57,301 --> 00:33:09,485
Perhaps, on nights like this one, he is already thinking about it, as he turns the scroll slowly in the lamplight, and lets his eyes wander from star to star.

220
00:33:11,285 --> 00:33:16,734
Down in the harbour, the merchant ship from Rhodes lies quiet at the quay.

221
00:33:16,734 --> 00:33:22,184
The captain is asleep in his cabin, with the empty cedar chest beside him.

222
00:33:22,184 --> 00:33:38,826
In a few days, his book will come back to him, a fresh copy, crisp and pale, and he will read it on deck on the long voyage home, never knowing that tonight his old copy is lying in the hands of the man who measured the world.

223
00:33:40,626 --> 00:33:43,499
The great library will not last forever.

224
00:33:43,499 --> 00:33:55,132
Over many centuries, through wars and changes of rulers and long years of neglect, it faded slowly away, and nobody knows exactly when its last scrolls were lost.

225
00:33:55,132 --> 00:34:10,642
But the work of its scholars, their catalogues, their careful copies, their measurements of the earth and sky, was carried onward, from library to library and from hand to hand, and some of it is still with us today.

226
00:34:12,442 --> 00:34:22,962
And in our own time, in the year two thousand and two, a new library opened in Alexandria, close to where the old royal quarter once stood.

227
00:34:22,962 --> 00:34:36,282
It is a great round building that slopes towards the sea like the disc of the sun rising from the water, its granite walls carved with letters from many alphabets of the world.

228
00:34:36,282 --> 00:34:41,882
The idea of a home for all the books, it seems, never quite went to sleep.

229
00:34:43,682 --> 00:34:49,940
Up on the roof, Eratosthenes has rolled the scroll closed and set it in his lap.

230
00:34:49,940 --> 00:34:51,817
His lamp has burned low.

231
00:34:51,817 --> 00:34:57,684
The breeze from the sea is cool and steady, and his eyes are growing heavy.

232
00:34:57,684 --> 00:34:59,639
Let yours grow heavy too.

233
00:34:59,639 --> 00:35:10,434
Let your breathing slow until it is as gentle as the small waves lapping against the harbour wall, rising and falling, rising and falling.

234
00:35:12,234 --> 00:35:20,770
The lighthouse keeps its fire burning out on the island, so that every ship at sea can find its way safely home.

235
00:35:20,770 --> 00:35:23,970
The scrolls lie sleeping on their shelves.

236
00:35:23,970 --> 00:35:30,524
The palms are still in the garden of the Muses, and the moon lies quietly in the pool.

237
00:35:30,524 --> 00:35:36,087
Thank you for spending this evening in Alexandria with The Quiet Archive.

238
00:35:36,087 --> 00:35:38,069
Sleep well, and goodnight.
